Repair the complete Rust source below and return a corrected complete `solution.rs`. The function parses and canonically reserializes a textual timeline, but entries sharing a tick are reordered incorrectly.

Keep the public API unchanged. Input consists of zero or more LF-separated records. A final LF is optional. Each record has exactly three tab-separated fields: an unsigned decimal `u32` tick, a category, and an escaped note. A category must be a nonempty sequence of lowercase ASCII letters, digits, or `_`. Within a note, `\\`, `\t`, and `\n` encode backslash, tab, and newline respectively; every other escape is invalid. Literal tabs and newlines therefore serve only as record syntax.

Parse every record, stably order records by ascending tick, and serialize them in the same canonical format without a final LF. Records with equal ticks must remain in their original relative order, even when their categories differ. Empty input produces an empty string.

Do not add a `main` function, tests, external packages, or environment-dependent operations.

Known-bad source:

```rust
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum TimelineError {
    WrongFieldCount(usize),
    InvalidTick(usize),
    InvalidCategory(usize),
    InvalidEscape(usize),
}

#[derive(Debug, Clone)]
struct Entry {
    tick: u32,
    category: String,
    note: String,
}

fn decode_note(raw: &str, line: usize) -> Result<String, TimelineError> {
    let mut out = String::new();
    let mut chars = raw.chars();
    while let Some(ch) = chars.next() {
        if ch != '\\' {
            out.push(ch);
            continue;
        }
        match chars.next() {
            Some('\\') => out.push('\\'),
            Some('t') => out.push('\t'),
            Some('n') => out.push('\n'),
            _ => return Err(TimelineError::InvalidEscape(line)),
        }
    }
    Ok(out)
}

fn encode_note(note: &str) -> String {
    let mut out = String::new();
    for ch in note.chars() {
        match ch {
            '\\' => out.push_str("\\\\"),
            '\t' => out.push_str("\\t"),
            '\n' => out.push_str("\\n"),
            _ => out.push(ch),
        }
    }
    out
}

pub fn normalize_timeline(input: &str) -> Result<String, TimelineError> {
    if input.is_empty() {
        return Ok(String::new());
    }
    let mut entries = Vec::new();
    for (index, record) in input.lines().enumerate() {
        let line = index + 1;
        let fields: Vec<&str> = record.split('\t').collect();
        if fields.len() != 3 {
            return Err(TimelineError::WrongFieldCount(line));
        }
        let tick = fields[0]
            .parse::<u32>()
            .map_err(|_| TimelineError::InvalidTick(line))?;
        if fields[1].is_empty()
            || !fields[1]
                .bytes()
                .all(|b| b.is_ascii_lowercase() || b.is_ascii_digit() || b == b'_')
        {
            return Err(TimelineError::InvalidCategory(line));
        }
        entries.push(Entry {
            tick,
            category: fields[1].to_owned(),
            note: decode_note(fields[2], line)?,
        });
    }
    entries.sort_by(|a, b| a.tick.cmp(&b.tick).then_with(|| a.category.cmp(&b.category)));
    Ok(entries
        .into_iter()
        .map(|entry| {
            format!(
                "{}\t{}\t{}",
                entry.tick,
                entry.category,
                encode_note(&entry.note)
            )
        })
        .collect::<Vec<_>>()
        .join("\n"))
}
